'''James Douglas Pillen''' (born December 31, 1955)<ref>{{Cite web |title=Jim Pillen - 1978 - Football |url=https://huskers.com/sports/football/roster/season/1978/player/jim-pillen |access-date=May 11, 2022 |website=University of Nebraska |language=en}}</ref><ref name=bio>{{cite news|title=Do what is right is first core belief of Pillen Family Farms|newspaper=Omaha World-Herald|date=October 7, 2018|access-date=June 5, 2023|url=https://omaha.com/special_sections/do-what-is-right-is-first-core-belief-of-pillen-family-farms/article_2101c569-79ce-570f-a519-4f32e47ee825.html|quote=...James Douglas Pillen completed their family as their third son}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Personals|newspaper=Columbus Telegram|date=February 22, 1975|access-date=June 5, 2023|url=https://www.newspapers.com/image/21266043/}}</ref> is an American [[politician]], [[veterinarian]], and [[livestock]] producer serving as the 41st and current [[governor of Nebraska]] since 2023.<ref>{{cite book |title=Ricketts Moves Close to Acknowledging Senate Interest |first=Don |last=Walton |date=November 21, 2022 |accessdate=November 21, 2022 |url=https://journalstar.com/news/state-and-regional/govt-and-politics/ricketts-moves-close-to-acknowledging-senate-interest/article_ee0e7810-37c3-5521-bdd9-f76eee486c25.html}}</ref> A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], Pillen served on the [[University of Nebraska system|University of Nebraska]] [[University of Nebraska–Lincoln#Board of Regents|Board of Regents]] from 2013 to 2023.

== Early life and education ==
Pillen was born in [[Columbus, Nebraska]], to Dale and Dorothy Pillen.<ref name=bio /> His parents were farmers.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Pillen Family Farms – Family Owned. Family Focused |url=https://pillenfamilyfarms.com/ |access-date=2024-08-07 |language=en-US}}</ref> Dale Pillen served in the [[United States Army]] during the [[Korean War]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite news |date=1999-02-01 |title=Obituary for Dale Daniel Pillen |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-columbus-telegram-obituary-for-dale/133760066/ |access-date=2024-08-07 |work=The Columbus Telegram |pages=6}}</ref> He died at a [[Columbus, Nebraska|Columbus]] hospital in 1999 at age 72.<ref name=":0" /> His grandfather immigrated to the United States from [[Germany]] and later served in the U.S. military during [[World War I]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Artley |first1=J. B. |last2=Farley |first2=David |last3=Schwandt |first3=Kevin |title=Nebraska Resource Center Ribbon Cutting |url=https://www.dvidshub.net/video/942594/nebraska-resource-center-ribbon-cutting |website=DVIDS |publisher=55th Wing |access-date=December 31, 2024 |language=en |date=October 31, 2024}}</ref>

After graduating from [[Lakeview Junior-Senior High School (Nebraska)|Lakeview Junior-Senior High School]] in 1974, Pillen earned a Bachelor of Science degree in animal science from the [[University of Nebraska–Lincoln]] and a [[Doctor of Veterinary Medicine]] from the [[Kansas State University College of Veterinary Medicine]].<ref name="BdofRegents">{{Cite web |title=Nebraska Board of Regents Member Jim Pillen|url=https://nebraska.edu/regents/board-members/jim-pillen |access-date=May 17, 2022 |language=en-US}}</ref>

From 1975 to 1978, Pillen was a [[defensive back]] for the [[Nebraska Cornhuskers football]] team under [[Tom Osborne]].<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Murtaugh |first1=Jerry |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=RwKKCgAAQBAJ&dq=Jim+Pillen&pg=PT68 |title=If These Walls Could Talk: Nebraska Cornhuskers: Stories From the Nebraska Cornhuskers Sideline, Locker Room, and Press Box |last2=Sheil |first2=Jimmy |last3=Rosenthal |first3=Brian |last4=Achola |first4=George |last5=Brashaw |first5=Brian |date=October 1, 2015 |publisher=Triumph Books |isbn=978-1-63319-330-7 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Williams |first1=Pat |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=ePgcQ6IQ754C&dq=Jim+Pillen&pg=PA34 |title=Tom Osborne On Leadership: Life Lessons from a Three-Time National Championship Coach |last2=Babcock |first2=Mike |date=December 26, 2012 |publisher=Advantage Media Group |isbn=978-1-59932-379-4 |language=en}}</ref> He was inducted into the Nebraska Football Hall of Fame in 2004.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Apr 7 |date=April 7, 2021 |title=NU Regent, Former Husker Football Player Jim Pillen Announces Bid For Governor |url=https://www.kfornow.com/nu-regent-former-husker-football-player-jim-pillen-announces-bid-for-governor/ |access-date=May 5, 2022 |website=KFOR FM 103.3 1240 AM |language=en-US}}</ref>

== Career ==
Pillen is a practicing veterinarian and also works as chair of Pillen Family Farms. The business, which employs members of Pillen's family, acquired DNA Genetics in 2003.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Founding Story of Pillen Family Farms and What Comes Next {{!}} Jim Pillen - Popular Pig |date=June 24, 2021 |url=https://popularpig.com/2021/06/24/the-founding-story-of-pillen-family-farms-and-what-comes-next-jim-pillen/ |access-date=May 5, 2022 |language=en-US}}</ref> Pillen has also worked as president of the Columbus Area Chamber of Commerce and chaired the Columbus Community Hospital Board of Directors.<ref>{{Cite web |last=McCartney |first=Rob |date=April 20, 2022 |title=University of Nebraska Regent Jim Pillen talks on his 'Pillen Playbook' and his strategy behind running for governor |url=https://www.ketv.com/article/university-of-nebraska-regent-jim-pillen-talks-pillen-playbook-strategy-behind-running-for-governor/39766591 |access-date=May 5, 2022 |website=KETV |language=en}}</ref> He has served on the University of Nebraska Board of Regents (which governs the [[University of Nebraska System]]) since 2012, and as vice-chair and chair in 2018 and 2020, respectively.<ref name=BdofRegents/>

Pillen was the Republican nominee in the [[2022 Nebraska gubernatorial election]].<ref name=CrowdedField>{{Cite web |last=Panetta |first=Brent D. Griffiths, Grace |title=Jim Pillen defeats crowded field and Trump ally to win GOP Nebraska governor primary |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/nebraska-governor-primary-charles-herbster-live-results-vote-counts-2022-5 |access-date=May 11, 2022 |website=Business Insider |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Epstein |first=Reid J. |date=April 30, 2022 |title=Nebraska Candidate for Governor Accused of Second Groping Incident at 2019 Dinner |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/04/30/us/politics/charles-herbster-groping.html |access-date=May 5, 2022 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> During the Republican [[Partisan primary|primary election]], he refused to debate his primary rivals.<ref>[https://nebraska.tv/news/election/pillen-says-no-to-primary-debates-thibodeau-says-he-should-drop-out-of-the-race Pillen says no to primary debates; Thibodeau says he should drop out of the race], NTV News (March 10, 2022).</ref><ref>Sara Gentzler, [https://omaha.com/news/state-and-regional/govt-and-politics/jim-pillen-turns-down-another-debate-rankling-fellow-gop-candidates-for-governor/article_bd3deefa-a0b9-11ec-bdd3-cfc9a19959a8.html Jim Pillen turns down another debate, rankling fellow GOP candidates for governor], ''Omaha World-Herald'' (March 10, 2022).</ref><ref>{{cite web|first=David|last=Siders|url=https://www.politico.com/news/2022/03/29/republicans-debates-midterm-00018253|title=Debate-dodging takes off in midterm campaigns|work=Politico|date=March 29, 2022|access-date=January 18, 2023}}</ref> Pillen was endorsed by incumbent governor [[Pete Ricketts]] and former governor [[Kay A. Orr]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Epstein |first=Reid J. |date=May 7, 2022 |title=In Nebraska, a Trump-Inspired Candidate Cracks Open Divide in the G.O.P. |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/05/07/us/politics/trump-endorsement-herbster-nebraska-midterms.html |access-date=May 11, 2022 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=January 18, 2022 |title=Ricketts endorses fellow Republican Jim Pillen for governor |url=https://apnews.com/article/college-football-sports-pete-ricketts-tom-osborne-nebraska-06508801bb69a73f4c0a4e505e4b341f |access-date=May 5, 2022 |website=AP NEWS |language=en}}</ref> In a crowded field of primary candidates, Pillen won the nomination with about 33.75% of the vote, defeating [[Charles Herbster]] (who received 30.13%), [[Brett Lindstrom]] (25.68%), and [[Theresa Thibodeau]] (6.05%).<ref name="Bauer_20220509"/><ref name=CrowdedField/> Pillen's running mate was former U.S. Attorney [[Joseph P. Kelly (attorney)|Joseph P. Kelly]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=April 5, 2022 |title=Republican Jim Pillen adds prosecutor Joe Kelly to ticket |url=https://apnews.com/article/2022-midterm-elections-donald-trump-nebraska-lincoln-campaigns-a1dbfa91da7bbb9da1f9eda2c42d8777 |access-date=May 5, 2022 |website=AP NEWS |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Epstein |first=Reid J. |date=May 11, 2022 |title=Jim Pillen upends a Trump-backed rival to win Nebraska's G.O.P. primary for governor. |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/05/10/us/politics/jim-pillen-nebraska-trump.html |access-date=May 11, 2022 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Pillen campaigned on [[Anti-abortion movement|opposing abortion]] and [[2020s controversies around critical race theory|critical race theory]].<ref name="Bauer_20220509">{{Cite news| last = Bauer| first = Will| title = Race results: Trump's endorsement fails in Nebraska GOP governor primary| work = NPR| access-date = 2022-05-11| date = 2022-05-09| url = https://www.npr.org/2022/05/10/1097588135/nebraska-primary-governor-election-trump-republican-gop}}</ref> In the general election, he refused to debate the Democratic nominee, [[Carol Blood]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Nebraskans Won't be Seeing Pillen Debate, Governor Hopeful Calls it 'Theater' |url=https://nebraskapublicmedia.org/en/news/news-articles/nebraskans-wont-be-seeing-pillen-debate-governor-hopeful-calls-it-theater/ |access-date=2022-10-05 |website=Nebraska Public Media |language=en}}</ref>

Pillen was elected governor with 59.2% of the vote to Blood's 36.0% and Libertarian nominee Scott Zimmerman's 3.9%.<ref name="official results"/> He took office on January 5, 2023. One week after being sworn in, he appointed his predecessor, [[Pete Ricketts]], to the [[United States Senate]] seat left vacant by the resignation of [[Ben Sasse]] to become president of the [[University of Florida]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/01/12/politics/pete-ricketts-senate/index.html |title=Nebraska Gov. Pillen appoints Pete Ricketts to Sasse's Senate seat|work=CNN|first=Eric|last=Bradner|date=January 12, 2023|access-date=January 18, 2023}}</ref>

== Governor of Nebraska ==
