Incumbent [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] governor [[Pete Ricketts]] was term-limited and unable to seek a third term.<ref name = "potentials">{{cite web |last1=Hammel |first1=Paul |title=Candidates exploring runs in 2022 governor's race; Ricketts can't run due to term limits |url=https://www.omaha.com/news/state_and_regional/candidates-exploring-runs-in-governor-s-race-ricketts-can-t/article_71a4b218-3453-5dd7-b0e9-7954bb988f11.html |website=omaha.com |date=November 3, 2019 |publisher=Omaha World Herald |access-date=4 March 2020}}</ref> Pillen won the gubernatorial election by a 23-point margin.

Nebraska's primary elections were held on May 10. Pillen won the Republican nomination, while [[Nebraska Legislature|state senator]] [[Carol Blood]] won the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] nomination.

The race took on increased importance in October 2022, when U.S. senator [[Ben Sasse]] announced he would resign and Ricketts said he would allow the winner of the 2022 gubernatorial election to appoint Sasse's replacement.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://nebraskaexaminer.com/2022/10/07/ricketts-punts-decision-to-next-governor-to-appoint-replacement-for-ben-sasse-if-he-resigns/ | title=Ricketts punts decision to next governor to appoint replacement for Ben Sasse if he resigns |work=Nebraska Examiner | date=October 7, 2022 }}</ref> Pillen appointed Ricketts to replace Sasse.

===Tenure===
[[File:Signing ceremony for LB 514 on June 1, 2023.jpg|thumb|200px|Governor Jim Pillen signs Legislative Bill 514 in his office, implementing photographic voter identification in Nebraska, as Senators [[Ben Hansen]] and [[Tom Brewer (politician)|Tom Brewer]] look on]]
In October 2023, Pillen drew criticism when responding to reporting about dangerously high levels of [[nitrates]] at his farms. Reporter Yanqi Xu had written that 16 Pillen farms had nitrate levels of at least 50 parts per million, five times the level considered safe to drink.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://flatwaterfreepress.org/governors-comments-about-ffp-reporter-are-in-a-word-wrong/ | title=Governor's comments about FFP reporter infuriating, dead wrong |work=Flatwater Free Press | date=October 17, 2023 }}</ref> Claiming not to have read Xu's article, he said: "all you've got to do is look at the author. Author's from Communist China—what more do you need to know?"<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/10/18/us/jim-pillen-nebraska-reporter-china.html | title=Nebraska Governor Is Criticized for Comments on Reporter's Chinese Nationality  |work=New York Times | date=October 18, 2023 }}</ref> The [[Asian American Journalists Association]] issued a statement condemning Pillen's remarks, writing, "Xu, an investigative reporter who grew up in China, deserves to do her job without being judged because of her nationality."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aaja.org/2023/10/18/aaja-stands-with-flatwater-free-press-journalist-yanqi-xu-who-was-the-target-of-comments-about-her-nationality/|title=AAJA stands with Flatwater Free Press journalist Yanqi Xu, who was the target of comments about her nationality|first=Yi-Shen|last=Loo|newspaper=Asian American Journalists Association|date=2023-10-18 |access-date= October 20, 2023}}</ref>

After announcing his intent to do so in his closing address to the 108th Legislature at its April adjournment,<ref>Wendling, Zach. "Nebraska lawmakers adjourn, but break will be short-lived as special session looms this summer." ''Nebraska Examiner''. April 18, 2024. [https://nebraskaexaminer.com/2024/04/18/nebraska-lawmakers-adjourn-but-break-will-be-short-lived-as-special-session-looms-this-summer/]</ref> Pillen called a special session of the legislature in late July to address property taxes.<ref>"How Nebraska’s special legislative session on taxes came about and what to expect." ''Associated Press''. July 31, 2024. [https://apnews.com/article/nebraska-special-session-taxes-f181dad33c8d31aeaedf9457d2b9525a]</ref> A number of state senators expressed frustration at the call for a special session and with the proposals presented, some of which were introduced at Pillen's request.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Knapp |first=Fred |last2=Media |first2=Senior Reporter/Producer Nebraska Public |title=Special session debate: Should there even be a special session? |url=https://nebraskapublicmedia.org/en/news/news-articles/special-session-debate-should-there-even-be-a-special-session/ |access-date=2025-08-20 |website=Nebraska Public Media |language=en}}</ref>

On August 19, 2025, Pillen and U.S. Secretary of Homeland Security [[Kristi Noem]] announced that an [[Immigration detention in the United States|immigration detention center]] called [[Cornhusker Clink]] would be established in [[McCook, Nebraska]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Funk |first=Josh |date=2025-08-19 |title=Nebraska announces plan for immigration detention center dubbed the 'Cornhusker Clink' |url=https://apnews.com/article/nebraska-ice-detention-center-immigration-1ae76a48f62a7304da63e2780a357190 |access-date=2025-08-20 |website=[[AP News]] |language=en}}</ref> The facility is planned to include 280 beds and will be in the [[Work Ethic Camp]], a minimum-security prison labor camp in McCook.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Pequeño |first=Antonio IV |title='Cornhusker Clink': Nebraska Says It Will Build 280-Bed Immigration Detention Facility |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/antoniopequenoiv/2025/08/19/nebraska-planning-cornhusker-clink-as-trumps-latest-immigration-detention-center-what-to-know/ |access-date=2025-08-20 |website=Forbes |language=en}}</ref> Development came after the [[Second presidency of Donald Trump|Trump administration]] pushed other immigration detention centers, such as [[Alligator Alcatraz]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Tedesco |first=Maria |date=2025-08-19 |title='Cornhusker Clink,' Trump admin to open new migrant detention center in Nebraska |url=https://13wham.com/news/nation-world/cornhusker-clink-trump-admin-to-open-new-migrant-detention-center-nebraska-alligator-alcatraz-deportation-depot-coyote-compound-lonestar-lockup-aclu-trump-administration-tom-homan |access-date=2025-08-20 |website=[[WHAM-TV]] |language=en}}</ref> The announcement immediately drew criticism from many Nebraska lawmakers and the Nebraska [[American Civil Liberties Union|ACLU]].<ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=2025-08-19 |title='Cornhusker Clink': Nebraska politicians, organizations react to Pillen announcement |url=https://www.wowt.com/2025/08/19/cornhusker-clink-nebraska-politicians-organizations-react-pillen-announcement/ |access-date=2025-08-20 |website=[[WOWT]] |language=en}}</ref>

Pillen was criticized<ref>Parsons, McKenzy [https://www.ketv.com/article/nebraska-governor-faces-criticism-over-comments/70115427 "Gov. Jim Pillen faces criticism over term he used during his tele-town hall"] ''KETV'', January 23, 2026. Retrieved January 25, 2026</ref><ref>[https://omaha.com/opinion/letters/article_d132acf6-e5fc-4d5c-b921-0c5eca7ceeaf.html "Public Pulse: Readers say they're outraged, ashamed by Pillen's 'libtard' comment"] ''Omaha World-Herald'', January 25, 2026. Retrieved January 25, 2026</ref> for repeatedly referring to [[Modern liberalism in the United States|liberals]] as "libtards" during a January 20, 2026, telephone town hall.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://nebraskapublicmedia.org/en/news/news-articles/nebraska-governor-takes-jabs-at-libtards-champions-conservative-wins-in-campaign-call/|title=Nebraska governor takes jabs at 'libtards,' champions conservative wins in campaign call|last=Ourada|first=Jackie|publisher=[[Nebraska Public Media]]|quote=Pillen also spent several instances during the telephone town hall taking jabs at people who criticize him, repeatedly using the term "libtard," which is often seen as a disparaging description of someone who has left-leaning political views.|date=January 20, 2026|accessdate=January 21, 2026}}</ref> He did not respond to media inquiries about his use of the [[pejorative]].<ref>Dvorak, Gina [https://www.wowt.com/2026/01/24/pillen-dodges-questions-about-town-hall-comments-no-bid-contract/ "Pillen dodges questions about town hall comments, no-bid contract"] ''WOWT'', January 23, 2026. Retrieved January 20, 2026</ref><ref>Hudson, Molly [https://www.3newsnow.com/news/local-news/gov-pillen-refuses-question-regarding-comments-in-tuesdays-telephone-town-hall"Gov. Pillen refuses question regarding comments in Tuesday's telephone town hall"] ''3NewsNow'', January 23, 2026. Retrieved January 20, 2026</ref>

In September 2026, ''[[The New York Times]]'' and The Flatwater Free Press, an independent, nonprofit newsroom in Nebraska, reported that at least twenty hog farms owned by Pillen were employing undocumented immigrants, according to court documents and interviews with current and former workers and managers.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Xu |first=Yanqi |date=2026-09-09 |title=Gov. Pillen backs ICE crackdown. His company employs undocumented workers. |url=https://flatwaterfreepress.org/gov-pillen-backs-ice-crackdown-his-company-employs-undocumented-workers/ |access-date=2026-09-11 |website=Flatwater Free Press |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Xu |first=Yanqi |date=2026-09-09 |title=As He Backs ICE Crackdown, Nebraska Governor Employs Undocumented Workers |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/09/us/jim-pillen-ice-nebraska-undocumented-workers.html |access-date=2026-09-11 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

== Personal life ==
Pillen and his wife, Suzanne, have four children and seven grandchildren.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gentzler |first=Sara |title=Does having kids matter in Nebraska governor race? Pillen and Herbster disagree |url=https://omaha.com/news/state-and-regional/govt-and-politics/does-having-kids-matter-in-nebraska-governor-race-pillen-and-herbster-disagree/article_3d79ea2c-7dee-11ec-861c-5fb6cbc127fc.html |access-date=May 11, 2022 |website=Omaha World-Herald |date=January 25, 2022 |language=en}}</ref> Pillen is [[Catholic Church|Catholic]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Jim Pillen |url=https://www.jimpillen.com/about/ |access-date=September 19, 2022 |website=jimpillen.com |language=en}}</ref> In December 2024, Pillen was hospitalized after sustaining serious but non-life-threatening injuries in a horseback riding accident in Columbus, Nebraska.<ref>{{cite web |title=Nebraska governor hospitalized after he is bucked off a horse |url=https://apnews.com/article/nebraska-governor-pillen-horse-a0b7507c2db5c287d2e6b2065d72f07f |website=AP News |publisher=Associated Press |access-date=25 December 2024 |language=en |date=23 December 2024}}</ref>

